<article class="env-lemma" id="07QN">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07QN">Lemma <span data-tag="07QN">15.46.2</span>.</a> Let $(R, \mathfrak m, \kappa )$ be a local ring. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$R \to R^ h$, $R^ h \to R^{sh}$, and $R \to R^{sh}$ are formally étale, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R \to R^ h$, $R^ h \to R^{sh}$, resp. $R \to R^{sh}$ are formally smooth in the $\mathfrak m^ h$, $\mathfrak m^{sh}$, resp. $\mathfrak m^{sh}$-topology. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Part (1) follows from the fact that $R^ h$ and $R^{sh}$ are directed colimits of étale algebras (by construction), that étale algebras are formally étale (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00UR" data-tag="00UR">10.150.3</a>), and that colimits of formally étale algebras are formally étale (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/031N" data-tag="031N">10.150.4</a>). Part (2) follows from the fact that a formally étale ring map is formally smooth and Lemma <a href="/tag/07EC" data-tag="07EC">15.38.2</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>